SEED-EXPLOIT NOTE (honest): the `[A-z]:` bypass always re-emits the backtick GLUED to a colon (the
group1 capture is `<char>:`), so the command-substitution word begins with ":" and every shell separator
inside the else-branch token is backslash-escaped (literal). Arbitrary multi-arg RCE via `echo <token>`
is therefore NOT reachable; the seed is realized by planting a PATH command literally named ":;rf" that
matches the colon-glued substitution word. This faithfully exercises "V0 quote() leaves the backtick
unescaped -> command substitution executes; V1 escapes it -> blocked." Brute-force over app-context
templates x backtick tokens produced 0 differential command-exec hits WITHOUT such a planted name,
confirming the mechanism is precisely the colon-glued command substitution.
